      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Background",
          "content": "Physician-assisted suicide (PAS) laws have been enacted in five US States and, along with physician-administered euthanasia, in Canada and the Netherlands."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Sources of data",
          "content": "Annual reports of the Oregon Health Authority and published research papers."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Areas of agreement",
          "content": "Not all recipients of lethal drugs use them to end their lives. Improvements in palliative care provision."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Areas of controversy",
          "content": "Rising numbers of deaths from PAS. Emergence of 'doctor shopping' and multiple-prescribing. Absence of qualitative scrutiny of assessment process. No re-assessment or oversight when prescribed drugs are ingested. Recent pressures to extend Oregon's PAS law."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Growing points",
          "content": "Reasons given for seeking PAS indicate this is a societal rather than a clinical issue and raise the question whether adjudicating on requests for legalized PAS is an appropriate role for doctors."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Areas for timely research",
          "content": "Research into quality of decision-making in requests for PAS and into potential role of doctors as expert witnesses rather than judges in requests for PAS."
        }
      ]},
